Given a photograph of a piece of rubbish, sort it into one of six materials:
cardboard, glass, metal, paper, plastic, or trash. The dataset is the Kaggle
garbage classification set; the question was how far classical methods get before a
convolutional network earns its cost.

## Three models on the same problem

| Model | Test accuracy |
| --- | --- |
| Support vector machine | 54% |
| Random forest | 56% |
| Convolutional network | **74%** |

The two classical models land within a couple of points of each other, which is its
own result: the random forest's extra flexibility bought almost nothing, because the
limiting factor was the features, not the classifier. The CNN, learning its own
features, gained eighteen points over the better of them.

## Where it fails is the interesting part

The per-class numbers say more than the headline. Paper is nearly solved — 0.97
recall — while **glass is the hardest class at 0.50 recall**, and most of what it
loses goes to metal, which correspondingly has the worst precision at 0.56. The
confusion is not random: photographed under the same lighting, a crushed glass
fragment and a crumpled can share their strongest visual cues, and a model working
from a few hundred training images per class has little to separate them.

The repository also holds the exploratory analysis and a Streamlit app that runs the
trained CNN on an uploaded photo.

